From: Anaconda recipe maintenance <pkg-team@anaconda.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Increase autograd python_functions codegen sharding 5 -> 20

A single generated python_functions_N.cpp shard costs 13-15GB to compile
with MSVC targeting arm64, which exceeds the 16GB PBP win-arm64 worker's
memory watchdog even with fully serialized compiles (five watchdog aborts
at the same TU cluster; see pytorch-feedstock#118). Quadrupling the shard
count brings per-TU compiler memory to roughly 3.5GB.

num_shards is a local variable consumed by both the python_functions.h
forward-declarations/init calls and the sharded .cpp writer, so the change
is self-consistent with no C++-side sync required (unlike the
python_torch_functions count, which must match gatherTorchFunctions).

Applied only on win-arm64 via a meta.yaml patch selector.
---
 tools/autograd/gen_autograd_functions.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tools/autograd/gen_autograd_functions.py b/tools/autograd/gen_autograd_functions.py
--- a/tools/autograd/gen_autograd_functions.py
+++ b/tools/autograd/gen_autograd_functions.py
@@ -568,7 +568,7 @@ def gen_autograd_functions_python(
     template_path: str,
 ) -> None:
     fm = FileManager(install_dir=out, template_dir=template_path, dry_run=False)
-    num_shards = 5
+    num_shards = 20
     fm.write(
         "python_functions.h",
         lambda: {
